<JourneyPost>
  <time datetime="2026-09-30">September 30, 2026</time>
  <h1>Where this starts</h1>

  <p>
    Every app we have built had the same shape. A database. An API in front of
    it. A UI in front of that. And between each pair, a layer of glue that
    restated what the layer beside it already knew: the fields, the rules, who
    may see what.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>FrontierJS starts from the other end.</strong> You write the schema
    — one <code>.lite</code> file — and the rest is derived from it. The model
    knows its fields, so the API validates them. The model knows who may read a
    row, so every query is filtered before it runs. The UI asks the same
    schema what a form needs, so the form is already right.
  </p>

  <h2>Three realms, one seed</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Data</strong> — the Model, declared in the schema, with access on it.</li>
    <li><strong>API</strong> — the Service, which a model gets for free and you extend.</li>
    <li><strong>UI</strong> — the Resource, the same data in the browser, live.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this journal is</h2>
  <p>
    FrontierJS is not released yet. It is pre-alpha, and we would rather show
    the work than announce it. These entries are the decisions, the dead ends
    and the things that broke — written while they are still fresh.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is coming soon. Until then, this is where you can follow along.
  </p>
</JourneyPost>
